---
import MainLayout from "../layouts/main.astro";
import ImgLoad from "../components/imgload.astro";
import img1 from "../imgs/img1.jpg";
import img2 from "../imgs/img2.jpg";
import img4 from "../imgs/img4.jpg";
import img5 from "../imgs/img5.jpg";
import img6 from "../imgs/img6.jpg";
import img7 from "../imgs/img7.jpg";
import img8 from "../imgs/img8.jpg";
---

<MainLayout title="Emily D'Silva">
    <div class="group">
        <p class="title">MIG Welding</p><p>
            At my job as a manufacturing engineering co-op at Martinrea I got to
            learn the fundamentals of MIG welding. I was able to get consistent
            weld lines without any burn-through. As a final project I sculpted a
            scorpion out of nuts, bolts, screws, and washers.
        </p>
        <div class="contentbox">
            <ImgLoad src={img1} width={350} alt="" />
            <ImgLoad src={img2} width={350} alt="" />
        </div>
    </div><br />
    <hr />
    <div class="group">
        <p class="title">Redesign of Ejection System</p>
        <p>
            While working at Martinrea I redesigned the part ejection systems
            for 9 different assembly substations. The main issue was that the
            leveling spring had snapped and coiled around itself making it
            useless (shown in the first picture). Because of this the system
            would not return to the home position or the part would get caught
            on the pins which would cause an increase in cycle time.
            Additionally, the broken ejector systems caused <u>160 minutes</u> of
            downtime over a 6-month period.
        </p>
        <div class="contentbox">
            <ImgLoad src={img4} width={350} alt="" />

            <ImgLoad src={img5} width={350} alt="" />
        </div>
        <p>
            The solution I designed to resolve these issues was to replace the
            mechanical spring with a long-life gas spring (shown in the second
            picture). This made the part ejection smoother, more reliable, and
            more durable. I designed a new mounting block for the bottom of the
            spring and modified the design of the bracket at the top to fit the
            spring attachment. I installed the new system into the substations
            and ran tests until I was satisfied with the process.
        </p>
    </div><br />
    <hr />
    <div class="group">
        <p class="title">Part Catcher for Critical Safety Issue</p>
        <p>
            The edges of the part dropoff chute (shown below) were not high
            enough and parts would fall on the floor which stopped the machine
            cycle and was a major safety hazard. I designed a catcher bracket
            out of sheet metal and added an additional rubber sheet on the side
            to cushion the impact. I got a lot of the machine shop experience
            while making it and I got to use a variety of tools such as a plasma
            cutter, band saw, hydraulic press, and drill press.
        </p>
        <div class="contentbox">
            <ImgLoad src={img6} width={350} alt="" />
        </div>
    </div><br />
    <hr />
    <div class="group">
        <p class="title">Stud Feeder Detection Redesign</p>
        <p>
            Across 18 DAS (Drawn Arc Stud) units there was constant jamming
            which caused considerable downtime. The inductive sensor shown in
            the first picture was not reliable and often caused the feeder bowl
            to keep dispensing more studs causing a waste of resources and a
            safety issue. To correct this, I decided to implement a laser sensor
            for more reliability. I designed and 3D printed a bracket to mount
            the sensor and I used the same mounting position as the inductive
            sensor so the installation process would not cause any downtime.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="contentbox">
        <ImgLoad src={img7} width={350} alt="" />
        <ImgLoad src={img8} width={350} alt="" />
    </div>
</MainLayout><br />
<hr /><br /><br />
